# Git & Version Control Standards

## Commit Message Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

[optional body - explain WHY, not WHAT]

[optional footer - references, breaking changes]
```

## Commit Types

| Type | Use For | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| `feat` | New capabilities | `feat(steering): add per-layer strength tuning` |
| `fix` | Bug fixes | `fix(memory): resolve activation tensor leak` |
| `exp` | Experiment changes | `exp(prompts): add injection-resistant test cases` |
| `docs` | Documentation | `docs: update CLAUDE.md with new workflow` |
| `refactor` | Code restructure | `refactor(hooks): extract common activation capture` |
| `perf` | Performance | `perf: batch activation extraction for 3x speedup` |
| `test` | Test changes | `test: add statistical validation for steering` |
| `chore` | Maintenance | `chore: update requirements.txt versions` |

## Scope Examples

- `steering` - Steering vector computation or application
- `activation` - Activation capture and storage
- `prompts` - Test prompt additions or modifications
- `viz` - Visualization and plotting
- `memory` - GPU memory management
- `stats` - Statistical analysis

## Pre-Commit Checklist

Before every commit, verify:

- [ ] No API keys or tokens in staged files
- [ ] No model weights or large binaries
- [ ] All notebook cells have been cleared of sensitive output
- [ ] requirements.txt updated if dependencies changed
- [ ] Code runs without errors

## Branch Naming

```
main                    # Stable, validated experiments
experiment/<name>       # Active research branches
fix/<issue>            # Bug fix branches
docs/<topic>           # Documentation updates
```

## What NOT to Commit

```gitignore
# Credentials
.env
*.token
credentials.json

# Model weights
*.bin
*.safetensors
*.pt
*.pth

# Large outputs
*.h5
*.hdf5
outputs/

# Jupyter checkpoints
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Cache
__pycache__/
.cache/
```

## Reverting Experiments

If an experiment goes wrong:

```bash
# See recent commits
git log --oneline -10

# Revert a specific commit (creates new commit)
git revert <commit-hash>

# Reset to previous state (destructive - use carefully)
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
```

## Tagging Releases

```bash
# Tag significant experiment milestones
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Initial steering experiment with 85% suppression rate"

# Push tags
git push origin --tags
```
